Q: How do I get my program to generate and print intermediary temporary arrays in a divide-and-conquer binary search? A: <p><code>mid</code> should be computed each time search space is reduced and <code>high</code> should be <code>len(array) - 1</code>. You're also changing data of the array with <code>array[mid] -= 1</code> and <code>array[mid] += 1</code>. What this is doing is taking the value of <code>array</code> at position <code>mid</code> and incrementing/decrementing the value by 1. Last it's generally bad practice to use while loops inside recursion. Here is the corrected example</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>def binary_search(array, item, low=0, high=None):
    if high is None:
        high = len(array) - 1

    if low &gt; high:
        print(f&quot;{array[low:high+1]}&quot;)
        return -1

    print(f&quot;{array[low:high+1]} (low={low}, high={high})&quot;)
    mid = (low + high) // 2

    if array[mid] == item:
        print(f&quot;Found {item} at index {mid}&quot;)
        return mid
    elif item &lt; array[mid]:
        return binary_search(array, item, low, mid - 1)
    else:
        return binary_search(array, item, mid + 1, high)


array = [2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 16, 17, 21, 32]
print(array)
item = 17

idx = binary_search(array, item)
print(&quot;Result index:&quot;, idx)
</code></pre>

